\n- Enforcing mutual exclusivity between `attack` and `kill` and restricting `block` to rally-ending stuff blocks cut invented events from 230 to 150 and raised mean OVERALL F1 from 0.701 to 0.735 (+0.034) in gen_02/cand_0.\n- Filtering out failed defensive touches after a point-winning kill and requiring a `dig` to keep an opposing attack in play cut invented events from 150 to 117 and raised mean OVERALL F1 from 0.735 to 0.759 (+0.024) in gen_03/cand_0.\n- Excluding soft free-ball passes/bumps from both `attack` and `dig` eliminated false-positive pairs in out-of-system rallies, cutting invented events from 117 to 82 and raising mean OVERALL F1 from 0.759 to 0.780 (+0.021) in gen_07/cand_1.\n- Wording in positive vs. negative definitions trades precision for recall: changing \"an opponent's attack\" to \"an opponent's spike\" in the dig definition caused missed events to jump from 33 to 54 (gen_05/cand_0, 0.773), whereas keeping \"attack\" and putting \"free-ball passes\" in the negative exclusion clause kept misses at 40 (gen_07/cand_1, 0.780).\n- Order of rule introduction matters: block/attack-kill rules (gen_01/cand_1, 0.660) and dig rules (gen_02/cand_1, 0.716) were rejected early on, but succeeded when stacked sequentially after set filtering in gen_02/cand_0 (0.735) and gen_03/cand_0 (0.759).\n- Providing explicit jersey number rosters and team colors consistently traded action/time accuracy for number accuracy: in gen_03/cand_3 and gen_04/cand_0, rosters boosted number F1 (+0.043 and +0.019) but increased invented events (+1 to +16) and lowered OVERALL F1 (-0.009 and -0.006 vs. baseline).\n- Even adding team shirt colors (\"white on left, blue on right\") without number rosters degraded detection, dropping OVERALL F1 to 0.750 (121 invented, 45 missed) in gen_07/cand_2 compared to the exact same attack rule without colors in gen_06/cand_0 (0.768 mean).\n- Forbidding `null` shirt numbers and instructing the model to inspect nearby frames for blurred numbers failed to improve number F1 (dropping from 0.590 to 0.584) and lowered OVERALL F1 from 0.759 to 0.751 in gen_06/cand_3.\n- Attempting to fix early serve timestamps (+1.1s to +1.5s bias) via prompt instructions\u2014whether anchored to hand contact (gen_01/cand_3, gen_02/cand_2, gen_03/cand_2) or net crossing (gen_04/cand_1)\u2014failed across four generations, consistently lowering OVERALL F1 (-0.007 to -0.023).\n- Enforcing multi-step sequential constraints\u2014such as within-possession contact sequences (gen_05/cand_3, 0.745), consecutive dig bans (gen_04/cand_2, 0.761), or one-serve-per-rally rules (gen_08/cand_2, 0.767, misses up from 40 to 59)\u2014degraded overall performance.\n- Over-tightening the set rule to explicitly ban sets before non-scoring attacks or dug spikes reduced or held flat invented events but increased missed events (+4 to +16), failing across four attempts (gen_05/cand_2, gen_06/cand_1, gen_07/cand_3, gen_08/cand_0).\n- Explicit dead-ball/post-whistle stopping rules and deflected-kill rules increased invented events when tested in isolation (138 invented in gen_05/cand_1 at 0.756; 135 invented in gen_06/cand_2 at 0.753 vs. 117 in gen_03/cand_0).\n- Single-run evaluations have ~0.01\u20130.02 noise (e.g., gen_04/cand_3 scored 0.777 then 0.766 on confirm; gen_06/cand_0 scored 0.780 then 0.768 on confirm), making multi-run confirmation essential before keeping a prompt.\n- Open questions worth testing: whether the promising stuff-block vs. kill distinction (gen_08/cand_3, 0.778 single-run) holds up when paired with simpler phrasing, and whether systematic errors that resist prompt changes (early serve timestamps, roster validation, sequential state constraints) should instead be handled via deterministic post-processing.\n"}, "parent_tiers": {"overall": 0.7878, "time": 0.8739, "action": 0.834, "team": 0.8298, "number": 0.6134}, "parent_counts": {"ok": 395, "wrong_team": 2, "wrong_action": 18, "missed": 40, "invented": 82}, "parent_per_type": 